      "i": 2,
      "beat": "concrete_example",
      "narration": "Redraw it honestly: 98.3 and 98.5, axis starting at 0. The two bars stand nearly side by side, and you'd have to squint to see which is taller. The difference is 0.2 points on a base of roughly 98. Maybe you start by imagining the bar as a physical object: the chart is saying one bar is three times taller, but the value is only 0.2% larger. So how did the first chart manage three times? Well, it only drew the part above 98.2. That leaves 0.1 for one bar and 0.3 for the other. Three to one, but that's a ratio of leftovers, not of the real quantities. And there's nothing special about 98.2. Any starting point near your data will exaggerate a small change, and the closer you cut, the louder it gets. This is the whole trick. Keep the numbers, shrink the base, and a two-tenths wobble looks like a skyrocket.",
