    {
      "i": 3,
      "beat": "visual",
      "narration": "Here's a way to catch this without redoing any arithmetic. Picture a wooden ruler laid against the y-axis. Mark where zero sits, and let the bar's length mean its value. On the honest chart, the ruler's end sits right at the base of the bars, and nothing is hidden. Now slide it up. The bottom edge climbs past the foot of each bar, and a chunk is sawed off. How big a chunk? Well, the ruler shows you: here it's about 98 units of a roughly 98.3-unit bar, nearly all of it. The bar you see is a sliver of the bar that exists. And there's nothing special about this chart; slide the ruler on any bar chart and you can measure what was cut. If you want a name for it, call this the ruler test. Does the bar's length still equal its value?",
