    {
      "i": 4,
      "beat": "generalize",
      "narration": "So is this just a crime-chart problem? Not at all. There's nothing special about crime or about bars. Picture a line chart of a stock, zoomed in on two weeks. It looks dramatic, a sharp climb. But zoom out and the same stock has gone sideways for ten years. The two weeks are real, but they're a sliver, like the sliver of bar we sawed off. Now picture a pie chart rendered in 3D. The front slice sits closer to you, so it looks bigger than a slice of the same size at the back. Different chart, different trick, same failure. What your eye reads as size isn't the size of the number. That's the family, and truncation is one member. Which gives you a rule that works anywhere: always ask what the visual size is proportional to. Length? Area? Distance from the camera? Then check whether that matches the value.",
